{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And whatever the unclean one touches.</b> This one who became unclean through a corpse — <b>it becomes unclean.</b>" "<b>And the person who touches</b> it: through the uncleanness of a corpse." "<b>It shall be unclean until evening.</b> From here we learn that a corpse is the av avot of tumah, and one who touches it is an av ha-tumah and renders a person tamei; this is its explanation according to its meaning and its halakhot. And from here I have copied from the foundation of Rabbi Moshe Ha-darshan, and this is it:" "<b>And they shall take to you.</b> From their own — just as they broke off the golden rings for the calf from their own, so they shall bring this for atonement from their own. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe Ha-darshan]" "<b>A red heifer.</b> A parable: to a maidservant's son who soiled the king's palace — they said, let his mother come and wipe away the filth; so let the heifer come and atone for the calf. (Tanchuma) [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe Ha-darshan]" "<b>Red.</b> After ״אִם יַאְדִּימוּ כַתּוֹלָע״ (Isaiah 1) — for sin is called red. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe haDarshan]" "<b>Without blemish.</b> After the name of Israel, who were without blemish and were made blemished in it — let this cow come and atone for them, and they will return to their perfection. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe haDarshan]" "<b>Upon which no yoke came.</b> Just as they cast off from themselves the yoke of heaven. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe haDarshan]" "<b>To Elazar the priest.</b> Just as they gathered against Aaron — who was a priest — to make the calf; and since Aaron made the calf, this service was not given to be performed by his hand, for the prosecutor cannot become the defender. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe haDarshan]" "<b>And burn the cow.</b> Just as the calf was burned. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe haDarshan]" "<b>Cedar wood and hyssop and scarlet wool.</b> These three species correspond to the three thousand men who fell because of the calf. And the cedar is the tallest of all trees and the hyssop the lowest of all — a sign that whoever became haughty and sinned must lower himself like the hyssop and the worm, and it will be atoned for him. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe haDarshan]" "<b>For a keeping.</b> Just as the sin of the calf is kept for generations for punishment — and there is no punishment in which there is not some measure from the punishment of the calf, as it says ״וּבְיוֹם פָּקְדִי וּפָקַדְתִּי״ and so forth (Exodus 32) — so too just as the calf defiles all who handle it, so the cow defiles all who handle it; and just as they were cleansed by its ashes, as it says ״וַיִּזֶר עַל פְּנֵי הַמַּיִם״ and so forth (ibid.), so too ״וְלָקְחוּ לַטָּמֵא מֵעֲפַר שְׂרֵפַת הַחַטָּאת״ and so forth. [Rashi in the name of R. Moshe haDarshan]"], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "מִ\"אַ (from here) apparently refers back to the verse or to this passage as the scriptural source for the halakhic classification of corpse-tumah."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:53.461461393Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}